bodyText:
You may include some or all utilities in the rent; the tagged clause `utilities-paid-by-landlord` lists them. Points of Virgin Islands law:
- (1) if you later charge the tenant separately for water or electricity, the charge must be based on the amount the tenant uses, and unless the lease says otherwise in writing you must install a meter if the tenant asks; a cap with overage billing is possible only on a meter serving the unit alone (see `utility-allowance-cap-vi`);
- (2) if you pass on a public sewer system user fee, charge no more than the actual fee attributable to the unit, divide a multi-unit fee equally between units, and never pass on penalties;
- (3) when the account is in your name, the Ratepayers' Bill of Rights protections (payment plans, disputed bills, medical holds) belong to you as the customer, and no statute protects the tenant if you fall behind, so keep your accounts current: your Housing Code duties to keep the water supply, pump, piping and wiring working still apply;
- (4) never cut off a service you supply to force payment or departure (see the Default & Termination topics on self-help).

No Virgin Islands statute says what happens to a residential lease when fire, a hurricane, flood or other casualty damages or destroys the unit. There is no statutory right for either side to end the lease, no statutory rent reduction, and no statutory rule on getting belongings out.

What does apply:
- (1) the Housing Code still requires an owner to keep a rented dwelling fit to live in, and the Housing Finance Authority can order repairs or order a unit vacated; the Department of Public Works can order an unfit dwelling repaired, or vacated and closed;
- (2) a public-housing tenant displaced by fire, storm, flood or other disaster gets first preference to move back when the unit is livable (government-managed housing only);
- (3) during a Governor-declared state of emergency, rents can be frozen at their pre-emergency level and charging an unconscionable rent is a crime;
- (4) a tenant who caused the damage is liable for it.

Whether the tenant still owes rent for a unit that cannot be lived in, and whether either side may end the lease, are common-law questions the Virgin Islands Supreme Court decides; modern courts often excuse rent for a destroyed home.

Cautious course: stop charging rent for any part of the home that cannot be used from the date of the casualty, refund prepaid rent for that time, let the tenant retrieve belongings as soon as it is safe, and put any agreement to end the lease in writing. If the tenant stays and you want possession, you still need a court order.

The optional `casualty-termination-vi` clause lets you end the lease after serious damage while keeping the tenant's rights.

No Virgin Islands statute lets you end a fixed-term lease early because you or a relative want to move in; that right comes only from the lease, and the library's optional clause `owner-occupancy-termination-ar` provides it. A month-to-month or other periodic tenancy can be ended by written notice equal to the rent interval. The rent-control subchapter, whose status is unsettled, adds three rules if it applies:
- (1) you may recover possession if you seek it in good faith for your own personal use (the statute does not mention relatives, and a sale is not a ground);
- (2) on the tenant's application the court must stay the eviction for up to six months at a time if the tenant has made reasonable efforts but cannot find suitable housing, or would suffer extreme hardship, and applies in good faith, with the tenant paying a reasonable rent into court or to you during the stay;
- (3) after recovering the unit for your own use (or for demolition) you may not later rent it to anyone else unless you first send the evicted tenant, by registered mail (or by posting on the premises if the tenant's whereabouts are unknown), a written offer to resume the tenancy at the same rent, which the tenant may accept in writing by registered mail within 30 days.

The restriction has no end date. A violation makes you liable to each evicted tenant for double the rent received from the new occupant or $50, whichever is greater, plus damages if the tenancy resumes.

Cautious course: give at least 30 days' written notice to quit, state the owner-use ground, move in yourself in good faith, and keep proof; if you later re-let, send the registered-mail offer first.

Virgin Islands law requires these landlord-tenant notices to be in writing:
- (1) every notice to quit, served by delivering it to the tenant or leaving it at the premises;
- (2) notice ending a tenancy at will or by sufferance (three months, or the rent interval if rent is payable more often);
- (3) the 14 days' notice to quit for unpaid rent on a tenancy at will;
- (4) if the rent-control subchapter applies, the 30 days' notice to quit before any possession case, and the registered-mail offer to re-let after an owner-use or demolition eviction; and
- (5) from the tenant, the at least 14 days' notice ending the lease as a domestic violence victim, with a police report.

No statute requires a tenant's repair request or a landlord's entry notice to be written, but put every notice in writing, dated, and keep a copy. An electronic record satisfies a writing requirement only where both parties agreed to deal electronically, and never replaces hand delivery of a notice to quit.
